## 3. Required Structure for Every Python Topic

Every topic must follow this structure.

### A. Topic Title

Display the topic name clearly.

### B. Meaning

Explain what the topic means in simple English.

### C. Why It Is Used

Explain why programmers use this concept and where it is useful.

### D. Learning Objectives

List what the learner will understand after completing the lesson.

### E. Detailed Explanation

Explain the concept step by step using beginner-friendly language.

Explain technical words whenever they appear.

### F. Syntax

Show the correct Python syntax in a code block.

Explain the purpose of each important part of the syntax.

### G. Example Program

Provide a complete Python program.

### H. Output

Show the expected output of the program.

### I. Line-by-Line Explanation

Explain each important line of the code.

### J. Real-World Example

Explain how the concept is used in a real application.

### K. Common Mistakes

Show common errors and explain how to avoid them.

### L. Interview Questions and Answers

Provide topic-specific interview questions with clear answers.

Include beginner, intermediate, and advanced questions when appropriate.

### M. Practice Questions

Provide exercises for the learner.

### N. Coding Challenges

Include small programming challenges with clear requirements.

### O. Solutions

Provide correct solutions with explanations, preferably in a collapsible section so learners can attempt the exercises first.

### P. Lesson Summary

Summarize the important concepts.

### Q. Mark as Completed

Allow learners to track completed lessons.

Do not skip these sections for important topics.

For advanced topics, explain prerequisite concepts before introducing difficult material.

